**Call Graph**

World snapshot nodes first enter the snapshot node tree, not `ClientWorld`:

`WorldSnapshot::load()` starts incremental `.ws` load and sets `ms_parsePending=true` / `ms_parsePhase=PP_wsNodes` at `src/engine/client/library/clientGame/src/shared/core/WorldSnapshot.cpp:702-737`. `WorldSnapshot::loadStep()` later advances through `PP_wsNodes`, `PP_buildout`, then `PP_sphereTree` at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:970-1050`. In `PP_sphereTree`, each root node is inserted into `WorldSnapshot`’s own `ms_sphereTree` by `node->setSpatialSubdivisionHandle(ms_sphereTree.addObject(node))` when the predicate passes at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1020-1040`.

Streaming creation path:

`WorldSnapshot::update(cellProperty, position_w)` refuses to create while parse is pending at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1193-1196`, skips a stationary/no-pending update at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1198-1199`, queries `ms_sphereTree.findInRange(position_w, 1.f, ms_queryList)` at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1214-1218`, diffs `ms_queryList` against `ms_loadedList` into `ms_pendingCreateList` / `ms_pendingDeleteList` at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1231-1281`, drains pending creates at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1328-1365`, then calls file-scope `createObject(ms_reader, node, result)` at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1363-1366`. On success it calls `addObjectToWorld(object, node)` at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1367-1370`.

`addObjectToWorld()` calls `object->addToWorld()`, marks the node in-world, and pushes the node into `ms_loadedList` at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:371-378`.

`Object::addToWorld()` early-returns if `m_inWorld` is already true at `src/engine/shared/library/sharedObject/src/shared/object/Object.cpp:1070-1076`; otherwise it sets `m_inWorld=true`, adds appearance/properties, and dispatches notifications via `m_notificationList->addToWorld(*this)` at `Object.cpp:1078-1092`.

For a snapshot POB/building: `ClientBuildingObjectTemplate::createObject()` returns `new BuildingObject(this)` at `src/engine/client/library/clientGame/src/shared/objectTemplate/ClientBuildingObjectTemplate.cpp:126-128`; `BuildingObject` derives through `TangibleObject(newTemplate)` at `src/engine/client/library/clientGame/src/shared/object/BuildingObject.cpp:29-30`; `TangibleObject` calls `ClientObject(newTemplate)` with no alternate notification at `src/engine/client/library/clientGame/src/shared/object/TangibleObject.cpp:263-265`; `ClientObject`’s default notification is `ClientWorld::getTangibleNotification()` at `src/engine/client/library/clientGame/src/shared/object/ClientObject.h:80-83`.

That notification is `ClientWorld::TangibleNotification`. Its `addToWorld()` inserts into `ms_tangibleSphereTree` only if `!object.isChildObject()` and `object.getSpatialSubdivisionHandle() == 0`; then it calls `World::addObject(&object, WOL_Tangible)` and `object.setSpatialSubdivisionHandle(ms_tangibleSphereTree.addObject(&object))` at `src/engine/client/library/clientGame/src/shared/core/ClientWorld.cpp:375-388`.

Other `WorldSnapshot` creation call sites using the same `createObject()` funnel:

`WorldSnapshot::update()` also creates visible-cell children when visible cells are non-world, their owner is in-world, the cell node exists and is not in-world, and each child id is not already live at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1402-1431`.

`WorldSnapshot::addObject()` finishes parsing if needed, adds a node and cell children to `ms_reader`, calls `createObject()`, and directly calls `object->addToWorld()` at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1508-1554`; unlike `addObjectToWorld()`, this path does not push `ms_loadedList` in the shown code at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1547-1554`.

`WorldSnapshot::loadIfClientCached()` is guarded by `networkId.getValue() <= int max`, finishes parse on a partial miss, climbs to the parent node, calls `createObject()`, then `addObjectToWorld()` at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1682-1703`.

`WorldSnapshot::addEventObjects()` requires `ms_eventObjectMap` nonempty and the event key present, then calls `createObject()` and `addObjectToWorld()` at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1794-1817`; on failure it removes the node from the snapshot sphere tree and clears its handle at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1818-1823`.

Editor paths also use the same funnel: `engine_wsAddObject` adds a top-level node to `ms_sphereTree`, calls `createObject()`, rolls back on failure, and calls `addObjectToWorld()` on success at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:2446-2488`. `engine_wsAddNodeAt` adds top-level nodes to `ms_sphereTree` for later streaming at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:2538-2554`, and immediately spawns contained children only if the container object exists and is in-world at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:2555-2574`.

**Insertion Sites Into `ms_tangibleSphereTree`**

There are three direct insertion sites:

`ClientWorld::TangibleNotification::addToWorld()` inserts newly world-added tangible objects at `ClientWorld.cpp:375-388`.

`ClientWorld::TangibleNotification::positionChanged()` removes and re-adds an already inserted object when it is not a child and has a nonzero spatial handle at `ClientWorld.cpp:407-413`.

`ClientWorld::TangibleNotification::extentChanged()` removes and re-adds an already inserted object when it is not a child and has a nonzero spatial handle at `ClientWorld.cpp:427-433`.

**All ClientWorld Sphere Trees And Routing**

`ClientWorld` owns three sphere trees: `ms_tangibleSphereTree`, `ms_tangibleNotTargetableSphereTree`, and `ms_tangibleFloraSphereTree` at `ClientWorld.cpp:189-191`.

The tree is chosen by the object notification, not by `WorldSnapshot` directly. `getTangibleNotification()`, `getTangibleNotTargetableNotification()`, `getTangibleFloraNotification()`, and `getIntangibleNotification()` return the four static notification objects at `ClientWorld.h:257-280`.

Tangible notification routes to `WOL_Tangible` and `ms_tangibleSphereTree` at `ClientWorld.cpp:375-388`. Tangible-not-targetable routes to `WOL_TangibleNotTargetable` and `ms_tangibleNotTargetableSphereTree` at `ClientWorld.cpp:453-466`. Tangible-flora routes to `WOL_TangibleFlora` and `ms_tangibleFloraSphereTree` at `ClientWorld.cpp:531-544`. Intangible routes only to `WOL_Intangible`, with no sphere-tree insertion at `ClientWorld.cpp:604-620`.

A portalized building created through `ClientBuildingObjectTemplate` should route to the tangible tree because it constructs `BuildingObject`, which goes through `TangibleObject`, which uses `ClientObject`’s default tangible notification at `ClientBuildingObjectTemplate.cpp:126-128`, `BuildingObject.cpp:29-30`, `TangibleObject.cpp:263-265`, and `ClientObject.h:80-83`.

A portalized building could end up in another tree only if its created object class or notification were different. Established examples: `StaticObject` explicitly uses `ClientWorld::getTangibleNotTargetableNotification()` at `StaticObject.cpp:23-24`; `CellObject` uses `getIntangibleNotification()` at `CellObject.cpp:50-52`; flora creation adds `getTangibleFloraNotification()` at `SetupClientGame.cpp:232-235`. A POB building template doing that is not established from these sources.

**Removal And Re-Add State**

Normal tangible removal: `ClientWorld::TangibleNotification::removeFromWorld()` runs only for non-child objects, removes from `World`, removes the sphere-tree handle, then sets the object spatial handle to `0` at `ClientWorld.cpp:393-402`. That leaves later re-add allowed by the `getSpatialSubdivisionHandle() == 0` gate at `ClientWorld.cpp:382-388`.

Position/extent updates re-add only when the object already has a nonzero handle at `ClientWorld.cpp:407-433`; they are not a recovery path for a zero-handle object.

`Object::removeFromWorld()` dispatches notification removal before clearing `m_inWorld=false` at `Object.cpp:1111-1152`, so the tangible notification’s handle clear occurs during normal removal.

`ClientWorld::remove()` calls `World::remove()` and then, if any sphere tree remains nonempty, calls `clearSphereTree()` for each tree at `ClientWorld.cpp:889-914`. `clearSphereTree()` removes objects from the sphere tree but does not call `object->setSpatialSubdivisionHandle(0)` at `ClientWorld.cpp:238-251`. Therefore that cleanup path can leave an object with a stale nonzero spatial handle; a later `TangibleNotification::addToWorld()` would refuse insertion because its add gate requires `getSpatialSubdivisionHandle() == 0` at `ClientWorld.cpp:382-388`.

Snapshot-side removal from the snapshot node tree is separate: `WorldSnapshot::unload()` removes each node’s `ms_sphereTree` handle and clears it at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:479-489`; `WorldSnapshot::removeObject()` does the same before `ms_reader.removeNode()` at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1576-1591`; `WorldSnapshot::suppressObject()` does the same without removing the authored node at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1606-1618`; event removal does the same at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:1768-1774`; editor remove does the same for root handles at `WorldSnapshot.cpp:2663-2668`. These are node handles, not `Object::m_spatialSubdivisionHandle`.

